## Shapes, layouts, companions and masks · `sec:nrz:lc-evaluation-layouts`

**Definition (Supported LC shape classes)** · `def:nrz:lc-evaluation-shapes`

The shape classes are a bounded selection of one finite/open instance axis; a bounded Cartesian block of finite instance axes; either instance class crossed with a finite observation/query-sample axis; either instance class crossed with bounded requested selections of an exact branch/cycle family; and either instance class crossed with both a finite sample axis and a bounded requested branch selection. The combined product has logical cells (i,j,b), where i retains the complete original instance-axis tuple. Its checked maps project to (i,j) for sample inputs and original family outcomes, to i for instance inputs, and to b for exact branch parameters; only dependency-independent inputs may broadcast. Each value and companion mask uses its declared target-cell correspondence. Branch membership is qualified by the full (i,j,b), so different samples of one instance can retain different cycles. The original (i,j) outcome view publishes one family answer or its scoped unresolved outcome under (`rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-alternative-masks`); alternative cells do not become incompatible settled options on that original view. Instance axes may vary L, C, initial phase, time, voltage/current amplitudes or energy independently as indices, without declaring their physical sources independent. Axis rank and extents are checked parameters within existing capacities, not new fixed limits. Joint observation reduction preserves its complete j incidence. A branch family with different per-cell membership uses masks over a checked branch selection, with further tiles when materialization cannot fit; no rectangular padding becomes a physical branch. Exact infinite branch domains remain lazy descriptor families. Source components and covariance target axes are companion dimensions rather than extra independent LC instances. The combined product admits specialization only when its rank, component multiplicity, sample/branch extents, projection/broadcast maps and branch guards satisfy the checked shape and layout law. Dynamic extents and maps are validated on each application; specializing rank, component multiplicity, an unrolled sample/branch extent or a captured branch coefficient/guard changes the lowering key under (`def:nrz:tiles-streams-lowering-key`). Sample-specific membership masks remain application data and cannot justify reusing another sample's branch attachment.

**Definition (Supported LC layout classes)** · `def:nrz:lc-evaluation-layout-classes`

Dense scalar fields use separate contiguous component planes with row-major logical-cell order. Rectangular views use checked nonnegative strides and offset maps into published planes; zero stride is read-only broadcasting of a shared value/source, never an overlapping writable output. Sample and branch products, including the combined (i,j,b) product of (`def:nrz:lc-evaluation-shapes`), have explicit component-to-cell maps; gathering and compaction retain every instance, sample and exact branch association. A gathered branch layout uses bounded injective original-index maps into retained parent tiles; compaction produces disjoint dense planes and a reverse association. Shared read views and separate component bindings need checked overlap correspondence; writes are injective and disjoint. Arbitrary negative strides, overlapping destinations, unchecked host pointers and implicit ragged nesting are outside these classes and need an admitted copy/map or typed refusal. Actual strides, extents and occupations discharge the shape law on application; same dimensions alone do not authorize reuse (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-lowering-application`).

**Rule (Companion inventory by LC meaning)** · `rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-companions`

Every real value, temporary and prelude region has its filled mask and the deterministic-total and numerical-only error companions required by its reading law. An endpoint representation declares the ordered lower/upper components and their numerical errors; it cannot silently reinterpret a radius plane as endpoints. Frequency/period outputs retain product-root-reciprocal and pi discrepancies; phase/time outputs retain clock, phase, wrap and exact-index import discrepancies; scale/energy outputs retain every original amplitude/element companion. Shared source support and calibration parameters have their own regions, masks and source-map targets, broadcast through checked maps without cloning physical variables. Statistical requests additionally need the declared joint model, Jacobian parameters, covariance and cross-target blocks with their own parameter errors and operating-point assurance. Missing cross blocks are unknown, not zero; unsupported generic root moment composition retains (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-root-companion-boundary`). Statistical coverage never supplies deterministic support. Atomic output groups and matching target coverage follow (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-mask-coverage`), (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-mask-demand`).

**Rule (LC mask views and compaction)** · `rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-branch-layout`

Use the engine's packed masks (`def:nrz:tiles-streams-mask-storage`) for filled, positive/rest, cycle/branch, settled, undecided and refusal roles. These are separate graph roles, each with one bit per logical cell; exact branch parameters remain descriptor domains. Pointwise LC lambdas use mapped intersections of required input coverage; observation/horizon reductions use their complete declared incidence. Branch results remain mask views by default, including alternating noncontiguous L inputs. Apply (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-branch-choice`) only when known LC consumers and an admitted cost-model revision justify compaction or its checked copy/release sequence is the only feasible continuation. Account for scanning, prefix counts, maps, every companion and simultaneous old/new residence. Unknown future stream reuse supplies no assumed copying benefit. Compaction preserves original epoch/index/source/branch associations by (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-mask-compaction`), and a mask view retains every earlier region its continuation reads. Tile choice is exclusively (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-tile-choice`); changing size never changes the LC question or its error goal.

**Rule (Set-valued answers and disjoint outcome masks)** · `rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-alternative-masks`

A cell whose physical support crosses several cycles or includes both rest and positive energy can have a certified set-valued family without one selected physical branch. On the original demanded cell layout it has one published family answer, or an undecided outcome when the requested single-branch decision remains unresolved. Certified projection coverage is not a new native decision option (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-decision-correspondence`). Its alternative values/domain results occupy distinct selections (i,j,b) on the declared branch-product layout, each mapped back to the same original association. Thus one original cell is not simultaneously classified into incompatible settled-option masks. Outcome partition and mask unions always use the same declared demand correspondence under (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-mask-algebra`); overlapping physical enclosures neither duplicate a logical cell nor grant branch feasibility. Compaction preserves the original index together with its branch selection, while the original outcome view retains the complete family.

## Standing streams and node flush contracts · `sec:nrz:lc-evaluation-stream-nodes`

**Definition (Sample, epoch and standing-question nodes)** · `def:nrz:lc-evaluation-stream-graph`

An open-axis node, indexed sample symbols and one explicit epoch instantiate (`def:nrz:lc-stream-axis`). A sample input application binds the host's original index association, reported time and selected measurements with their companions; it creates no new LC relation per reading. The clock node relates actual time to reported time by the declared constant offset and optional independent timestamp interval; physical phase connects to that same epoch. Standing outputs instantiate the frequency, phase, inverse-member, scale or calibrated-future graph on each demanded selection. A per-reading phase threshold has local demands; observation recovery and a window horizon join the required samples across tiles. No physical time is inferred from axis order or host arrival time. Local-index rollover preserves the exact epoch/map and charges any new transport evidence at a barrier (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-clock-boundary`).

**Rule (Shared sources and ordered aggregate questions)** · `rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-stream-quantifiers`

For a retained joint source family S, local universal answers have scope `forall s in S, P(i,s)`. Complete indexed coverage can compose these into `forall i in D forall s in S, P(i,s)`; local witnesses can compose only into `forall i in D exists s in S, P(i,s)`. A common-source assertion `exists s in S forall i in D, P(i,s)` requires one full joint witness or equivalent existence proof, never separate cell witnesses. The initial aggregate repertoire supports these explicit forms and their witnessed refutations/exclusions when their original inner obligations are checked; other alternations return unsupported until a checked recipe is supplied (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-quantifier-order`). Shared d, phase offsets, gains and source model identities remain common across all tiles and windows. Narrowing S may remove a witness; domain transport follows (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-decision-scope`), (`rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-refinement-barrier`). Equal readings do not create shared sources, and changing a window does not reset them.

**Rule (Partial, late and corrected inputs)** · `rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-stream-inputs`

LC uses the host request/loan/handback operations of (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-host-loan`). Partial, sparse and empty tiles are ordinary inputs under (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-partial-tile`); unused capacity is no observation and produces no phase, energy or timestamp. A local phase answer needs its actual complete source and time demands, while a fixed observation solve keeps missing inventory positions explicit. Late or corrected readings enter as new input applications with host-declared original association and supersession relation under (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-host-inputs`). They never overwrite published output versions. A correction with checked inclusion can refine the same question; otherwise it changes assumptions and invalidates its actual live descendants. Historical exact theorems retain their old premises, with bounded restoration or unavailable history for recomputation (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-retirement`). There is no LC reordering frontier, automatic correction model, loss policy or deadline in the graph.

**Rule (LC input, prelude and numerical-node flush)** · `rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-flush-numerical`

Every LC node declares the interface of (`def:nrz:tiles-streams-node-flush`). A pending sample without a loan closes unproduced contributions as absent on notification; a lent sample remains mandatory until handback or cancellation. Pointwise frequency, phase, inverse-member, scale and energy lambdas normally require the mapped intersection of all their inputs and companions. After their producers terminate under flush, they compute that published subset and mark required missing selections unavailable, without a numerical placeholder. Observation intersections and other reductions require their declared complete operand inventory unless the question explicitly defines an observed-domain reduction. Prelude and compaction nodes run only for remaining selected consumers. Output/delivery nodes retain the result, mask, companion and evidence readers until release or acknowledgement. These LC transitions specialize (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-flush-native`); the engine owns their notification and release mechanics.

**Rule (LC local barrier flush)** · `rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-flush-local`

A local sign, energy, bracket, phase-cycle or threshold barrier normally becomes ready when its exact selected value/companion demands are published. Under flush, every admitted demanded producer must first publish or terminate with absence; outstanding loans and required in-flight kernels remain dependencies. The barrier then classifies each actually complete cell using its original domain, leaves holes absent and publishes undecided/refusal masks for participating unresolved/failed cells. A missing required coordinate yields an explicit unavailable/missing-input result for that selection, not a narrower silently substituted question. Permitted finite numerical tightening or covered physical subdivision may still run with remaining allowance and inherited flush. Output companions publish only on valid continuations; reader pins release after child uses and required delivery end, following (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-flush-local`).

**Rule (LC fixed-inventory solve and horizon flush)** · `rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-flush-inventory`

A recovery, full consistency, rest-inventory or calibrated horizon node declares all required original observation and source demands. Ordinarily it waits for that inventory. Under flush, producers must terminate, but missing observations cannot be omitted from the fixed question. A full feasible witness must satisfy every original constraint; a universal cover must cover every required contribution. A checked contradiction from an available subset may prove whole-question emptiness only when its exact implication excludes every completion of the missing constraints. Otherwise publish scoped undecided/missing input with the original candidate family, and optionally a separately declared observed-subdomain result. Complete already retained constraints and an original proof may settle a horizon without retaining all historical numerical cells, provided the evidence resolves under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-retirement`). Finite refinement is allowed on present evidence; flush cannot supply a missing measurement or a new calibration assumption.

**Rule (LC family, inverse-tail and future-ray flush)** · `rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-flush-family`

A family materializer waits only for its requested finite members and their proved family premises. Flush closes unproduced requested selections as unavailable once producers terminate; it does not convert the exact family domain into its rendered prefix. An inverse tail can settle from the complete overlap theorem without evaluating all later members; absent theorem premises leave an undecided tail with the full candidate family. A horizon can settle the whole declared future ray only from its extremum and source-law proof, not from the last arrived time. Any valid future-ray claim keeps the fixed assumptions under which it was proved and makes no claim about arbitrary future host corrections. Finite and infinite domain outcomes retain evidence while numerical start/horizon regions and all readers release through the ordinary delivery path. This is the LC specialization of (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-flush-quantified`).

**Rule (LC window and observed-domain aggregate flush)** · `rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-flush-aggregate`

An observed-domain aggregate closes the finite actual observation domain D at the notified generation and proves only that version. A fixed-window predicate instead retains its declared index/time window and exact boundary ownership, including uncertain timestamp membership. Under flush, a universal index assertion needs complete required coverage for yes or a feasible counterexample for no; an existential assertion needs a feasible witness for yes or complete exclusion for no. The inner physical/source quantifiers remain those of (`rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-stream-quantifiers`), so neither per-cell witness collection nor absence proves a common-source claim. Missing window membership or missing required readings leaves undecided/missing input. An empty observed index domain has its declared logical aggregate meaning, while an empty physical LC family has the separate impossibility outcome; neither manufactures a physical observation. A whole-future assertion stays open without independent proof. Multi-tile and sub-tile barriers use the same law and preserve outside readers under partial flush (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-flush-partial`).

**Rule (LC refinement drain and completed flush)** · `rule:nrz:lc-evaluation-flush-refinement`

Every activated refinement child inherits the parent's notification and remaining cumulative allowances under (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-flush-local`). Its normal and flushed readiness follow its own local, inventory or aggregate contract above. A completed child publishes valid tighter coverage or explicit unresolved obligations; failure retains prior evidence and releases unused continuation edges. Full LC flush can complete only under (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-flush-completion`), including release of shared numerical pi, source parameters, masks, companions, gathered maps and all old-tile readers. Eligible exact LC authority and lowerings remain warm within bounded retention, but no numerical payload survives as occupied heap. Partial flush reports its scope and surviving outside/shared occupations. Renewed input creates fresh applications and numerical imports; it does not revive retired attachments.

